# satellite 004 -- build output and scratch. Nothing here is source.
/build/
__pycache__/
# The number the last build used, per checkout (build_number.py), and its lock.
/.satellite_build
/.satellite_build.lock
*.o
*.so

# The author's timing test builds beside its source.
/polymorph/test
/polymorph/out.txt

# old_versions/second_satellite/ keeps its own .gitignore for 003's build output.

# The first satellite is kept on disk to read and DELIBERATELY NOT IN THIS
# REPOSITORY'S HISTORY -- the second satellite's .gitignore said why: a whole tree
# with its own history elsewhere, 85 MB. old_versions/second_satellite/ IS
# tracked: it was this repository until 2026-09-15.
/old_versions/first_satellite/

# THE PROTOTYPE IS TRACKED FROM 2026-09-16 (the author: "we can add prototype to
# the git now, i've added a readme file with it that explains what prototype is").
# It was excluded on the same "its own history elsewhere" grounds as
# first_satellite, and that reason did not hold: there is no .git inside it and no
# copy anywhere on disk, so it was in NO version control at all -- 307 files that
# one rm would have ended. Its __readme__ is the thing to read first, and it is an
# instruction, not a description: DO NOT PULL CODE FROM IT.
#
# ONLY ITS SOURCE IS TRACKED. 15.22 MB of the 17 MB was build output; *.o above
# takes the object files, and these take the eight test executables, which have no
# extension and would otherwise slip through. What is left is 0.70 MB of .cpp,
# .hpp and Makefiles.
/old_versions/prototype/*/test_lexer
/old_versions/prototype/*/test_parser
/old_versions/prototype/*/test_error_reporter
/old_versions/prototype/*/test_resolver
/old_versions/prototype/*/test_m[0-9]
/old_versions/prototype/*/test_m[0-9][0-9]

# The 16-bit bytecode saved beside a program (arguments.sate). A build
# artifact of a run, never a source: the .satl is what is edited.
*.sate

# vendor/ -- other people's source, fetched by vendor/<name>/fetch.sh and checked
# against upstream's own sha256sum (vendor/README.md). GTK unpacks to 201 MB and has
# its own history upstream, so the RECIPE is in git and the tree is not -- the same
# rule as old_versions/first_satellite above.
/vendor/*/*.tar.*
/vendor/*/*.sha256sum
/vendor/gtk-old/gtk-*/
/vendor/gtk-old/.mesonvenv/
/vendor/gtk-old/build-*/

# vendor/new/ REVERSES THE RULE ABOVE, and it is the author's, 2026-09-20: "we need no
# dependencies, if you need something, include a copy of it so it exists inside of this
# project folder". These 26 files ARE the project's dependencies -- every library that
# links into satl, frozen at one version, with SHA256SUMS beside them recording where
# each hash came from. `/vendor/*/*.tar.*` above would have ignored 24 of the 26, which
# is the old policy ("the RECIPE is in git and the tree is not") applied to a folder
# that exists precisely so the tree IS in git. Without this line a fresh clone gets the
# scripts and none of the source, which is the thing vendoring was meant to end.
!/vendor/new/*

# THE UNPACKED TREES ARE NOT IN GIT -- the tarballs they come from ARE (vendor/new/,
# above), so every one of these folders is reproducible with `tar xf`. Together they are
# ~590 MB of other people's source with its own history upstream, and committing both
# the tarball and its contents would store everything twice.
#
# WHICH MEANS AN EDIT TO VENDORED SOURCE WOULD NOT BE IN GIT EITHER, and would be lost
# silently the next time the tree was re-unpacked. So an edit is committed as a PATCH
# FILE beside its journal entry -- vendor/edit_journal/<project>/*.patch -- which keeps
# the tree reproducible, the change reviewable, and re-applying it a command rather than
# an act of memory. vendor/README_FIRST.md says this too.
/vendor/cairo/
/vendor/expat/
/vendor/fontconfig/
/vendor/freetype/
/vendor/fribidi/
/vendor/gdk-pixbuf/
/vendor/glib/
/vendor/gperf/
/vendor/graphene/
/vendor/gtk/
/vendor/harfbuzz/
/vendor/libepoxy/
/vendor/libffi/
/vendor/libjpeg-turbo/
/vendor/libpng/
/vendor/libtiff/
/vendor/libxkbcommon/
/vendor/meson/
/vendor/pango/
/vendor/pcg-cpp/
/vendor/pcre2/
/vendor/pixman/
/vendor/wayland-protocols/
/vendor/xkeyboard-config/
/vendor/zlib/

# vendor/stage/ -- the shared install prefix every project is built into. Build output.
/vendor/stage/

# What vendor/build_stack.py makes. The two .py files beside them ARE in git -- they are
# the recipe, the same rule as everywhere else in this folder -- and everything below is
# regenerated from them by one command:
#   build/            one meson/autotools/cmake build directory per project, plus the
#                     generated native.ini that blocks meson's CMake fall-through
#   build-cc/         the two-line -w compiler wrapper, written from --clang
#   build-tools-path/ one symlink, python3 -> /usr/bin/python3, so a generator that
#                     hardcodes `python3` does not run under this machine's alpha PyPy
#   build_logs/       one log per project; the record of a run, not of the tree
/vendor/build/
/vendor/build-cc/
/vendor/build-tools-path/
/vendor/build_logs/

# vendor/fonts/ -- IBM Plex Mono. Regular and OFL.txt ARE in git: the font is the
# satellite look (titles are IBM Plex Mono 11px, never bold, never italic) and a static
# satl must embed one, because a GTK4 binary with no font reachable exits 139 rather
# than drawing tofu. The other thirteen faces are dead weight for a language that never
# uses them -- `sh vendor/fonts/fetch.sh --family` gets them back.
/vendor/fonts/ibm-plex-mono/*.ttf
!/vendor/fonts/ibm-plex-mono/IBMPlexMono-Regular.ttf

# vendor/gtk build outputs: the experiment binaries (85 MB each, unstripped) and the
# meson install prefix. Both are made by the scripts beside them.
/vendor/gtk-old/hello/hello-static
/vendor/gtk-old/hello/hello-dynamic
/vendor/gtk-old/stage-static/

